*(
    title: Inheritance Patterns
    description: Predicting offspring from parents, and the crosses where the simple ratios stop working.
    tags: inheritance, punnett square, pedigree, sex linkage, module 5, biology, stub
    lesson
)

@include(src: ../preface.part.chalk)

scene{
    Mendel's ratios come out of a model simple enough to be wrong in interesting ways: two alleles per gene, one dominant, genes inherited independently. Most of the work is recognising which assumption a given cross violates.

    Written out here: monohybrid and dihybrid crosses and the Punnett squares that predict them, reading a pedigree backwards to infer genotypes, sex linkage and why it produces different ratios in males and females, and codominance and incomplete dominance where neither allele is masked.

    Scaling the same reasoning from one family to a whole population, and the technologies that read genomes directly, is *Population Genetics*.

    internote(ref: population-genetics.chalk)
}
